   214	| 15 | Tenant mix | One large tenant (1m entities) and 1,000 small tenants (1k each) under the same live load | storage-layer isolation; per-tenant pull and commit bars hold for large and small tenants | P1, P4 |
   215	| 16 | PII erasure request | Erase PII for 10k subjects (crypto-shredding or real deletion) during live traffic | values are unrecoverable from every layer; history keeps its shape; explain still works | P1, P3 |
   216	| 17 | Agent proposals churn | Agents write drafts and proposals at 5x the human rate, most discarded | proposals never leak into decisions; pulls at a basis see exactly what was committed | P2, P4 |
   217	| 18 | Recruiter dashboards | "Placements in this branch with an overdue I-9" lists with filters, sort and paging at 256 concurrent readers | answers come from maintained projections, match the oracle, and keep stable cursors while writes continue | P1, P4 |
   218	| 19 | Feed consumer crash | The dependency-index consumer crashes mid-stream and resumes from its checkpoint | no gap, no duplicate processing, per-subject order kept; catch-up lag measured | P3 |
   219	| 20 | Midnight expiry wave | 20,000 documents expire at midnight (valid-time boundaries) while morning traffic starts | every affected subject is re-run at the boundary; reads before and after midnight are exact; live p99 holds | P3, P5, P1 |
   220	
   221	**Numbers rule inside the scenarios.** Pay rates, hours and money use scaled integers or decimal strings with order-preserving
   222	encodings; scenarios include range rules such as "pay rate at or above $16.50" and "hours over 40", and at least one write of a
   223	value the engine cannot hold, which must fail with a typed error.
   224	
   225	### 6.1 World definition W0, the domain oracle and the added failure combinations (Codex findings 6 and 7)
   226	
   227	**W0 is a small frozen world definition** used by every scenario, the P5 sweep and the P3 dependency rules. It is synthetic and stands in
   228	for Onboarded's real world definition; it is not a product feature of Triplex.
   229	1. About 12 rules over the domain: I-9 Section 1 and 2 tasks with due times; E-Verify status; background-check result; document expiry
   230	   (valid-time boundaries); pay rate at or above the work location's minimum wage (a shared referenced entity, so a location change
   231	   affects every worker there); hours over 40 (overtime); start-date boundaries; proposals never count as decisions.
   232	2. Two releases, R1 and R2, where R2 changes three requirements; releases are content-addressed, and activation is a fact.
   233	3. It is implemented twice: the TypeScript reference kernel the drivers call, and O-DOMAIN, an independent stdlib-only Python oracle that
   234	   gives the expected next tasks, obligations with due times, work items and effect traces from the same command stream.
   235	4. Plan-impact preview (GS.11) and activation (GS.10) are compared at one common input basis, never at different bases.
   236	5. Every scenario carries positive witnesses (subjects that must change) and negative witnesses (subjects that must not).
   237	
   238	**Failure combinations added to existing scenarios.**
   239	| Scenario | Added schedule | Expected outcome |
   240	| --- | --- | --- |
   241	| GS.03 | A vendor's older revision of a status arrives after a newer one for the same subject | Acknowledged as `superseded`: no change to current values, recorded in the journal (provisional, flagged for Sam) |
   242	| GS.04b | The current index's feed consumer is cut for 60 s while the source keeps committing | Reads at bases beyond the index watermark wait or route to a fallback per section 4.1; none answers from the stale index |
   243	| GS.06b | A work location's minimum wage changes (one shared referenced entity) | Every worker at that location appears in the feed's affected-subject set and is re-run; no other worker is |
   244	| GS.15 | A tenant-A route attempts to read tenant B's storage directly; one tenant's feed consumer is slowed 10x | The access fails at the storage layer; other tenants' feed lag and p99 hold |
   245	| GS.19b | The consumer crashes after delivering an outbox item but before recording completion | The receiver deduplicates by the delivery key: exactly one externally visible effect |
   246	
   247	## 7. Erasure semantics (Codex finding 8; RULINGS Q24)
   248	
   249	1. **The boundary is the erasure commit's publication.** A read that started before the erasure published may return the old value; every
   250	   read that starts after it, at any basis including bases before the erasure, returns a typed erased marker (`ErasedValue {erasedAt}`)
   251	   in place of the value. Assertion identities and valid and record intervals keep their shape. (Provisional, flagged for Sam: explain at
   252	   a pre-erasure basis shows the marker, not the value.)
   253	2. **Reproduction holds modulo erasure.** Cursor stability, bit-for-bit explain and GS.12 compare answers with erased values replaced by
   254	   their markers.
   255	3. **Protected-copy inventory.** Every lane lists every place a protected value or anything derived from it lives: source log and journal,
   256	   current index, history index, analytical copy, order-key and lexical channels, the QLever broker's log of record, caches, checkpoints,
   257	   backups. Erasure must reach each one, or the value must be stored only under a per-subject key that is destroyed (crypto-shredding).
   258	4. **Derived channels count.** An order key reveals ordering, so a PII attribute's order key is either inside the encrypted envelope (range
   259	   rules on PII then run in the kernel after decryption) or the attribute is not eligible for range rules.
   260	5. **Restore re-applies erasure.** The erasure ledger is kept apart from the data and replayed after any restore or rebuild.
   261	6. **Tests:** a reader already pinned when the erasure lands; a rebuild after erasure; a restore from a pre-erasure backup; independent
   262	   post-erasure expected answers from the oracle.

