    18	## 2. What changed: performance targets and requirements
    19	
    20	1. **Sam's five priorities, in his order, are the hill-climb fitness** (`lab/NORTH-STAR.md`, fixed):
    21	   P1 many concurrent reads, each pinned to an exact point in time, that never block writers;
    22	   P2 small, atomic, idempotent commits per subject, with low latency;
    23	   P3 an ordered, resumable change feed;
    24	   P4 a fast path to current values, kept separate from history;
    25	   P5 release activation re-evaluating up to 100k subjects without hurting live traffic.
    26	2. **Their measurements, the fixed writer trace and provisional bars are in `lab/TARGETS.md`.** They replace the old
    27	   performance targets: the generic load bar (256 requests/s offered, 25.6 commands/s, latency bars), "10m entities and
    28	   100m facts" as the main performance target, and the old 256/512/1024-client load and stress profile with its frozen
    29	   overload policy. Concurrency of 256 readers (1,024 under stress) with a fixed writer trace running stays, as part of P1.
    30	   Year-one scale (10m entities, 100m active facts, 150M to 500M retained facts a year) is the size the priorities are
    31	   measured at, not a target in itself.
