    99	| Q22 decimal operations | Result types: addition and subtraction take scale max(s1, s2) and precision max(p1 - s1, p2 - s2) + scale + 1; multiplication takes scale s1 + s2 and precision p1 + p2; a result beyond the target's declared precision, or beyond the kernel maximum of 38 digits, is a typed overflow error. Division exists only as `divide(a, b, scale, rounding)` with an explicit scale and rounding mode (half-even, half-up, down): the one place rounding happens, and only when the caller names it; a bare `/` on decimals is a preflight error. Conversions: integer to decimal is exact when it fits the precision, else a typed error; decimal to integer only when the fraction is zero, else a typed error unless the caller writes `round(x, 0, mode)`; decimal to number only through `toNumber` (named, lossy); number to decimal only through `toDecimal(x, p, s, rounding)`. Scale evolution: widening precision or scale is a metadata change; narrowing is a migration that fails atomically if any value would lose digits. CMP-DECIMAL-CROSS: every comparison between decimal and integer or number, equality included, is a preflight error. The writer captures the caller's original typed input before any normalization, and tests assert nothing was rounded before capture. Tests cover adjacent large decimals, negatives, zero, scale boundaries, arithmetic intermediates, migrations, every query and serialization path, and an independent check of order keys against exact numeric order. | Sam's corrected numeric rule; Codex finding 9 |
