     1	# EXP-P2X XTDB per-subject commit path and change feed (tiny, 10k rung; includes CF01 for XTDB)
     2	
     3	**North star:** P2 (small atomic idempotent per-subject commits, low latency) first, P3 (ordered resumable feed) second. Measured as
     4	TARGETS 1.1 P2 (open-loop offer-to-published-ACK per size band, full command accounting) and P3 (durable affected-set record plus
     5	checkpoint, lag from source publication, three crash points, visible duplicate effects 0).
     6	**Scope:** lab/SCOPE.md, lab/NORTH-STAR.md, lab/TARGETS.md v2 (sections 1.1, 2.1, 4.1). Evidence to build on:
     7	lab/research/R16-xtdb-northstar-fit.md sections 2 and 3, lab/research/R18-read-path-feed-sweep-prior-art.md sections 3 and 5,
     8	lab/research/R14-xtdb-correctness-hazards.md. Inherited homebase files are for code facts only.
     9	
    10	## Commander's intent
    11	Commits on different subjects stop contending with each other while the feed stays gap-free and resumable. Today every commit in a
    12	scope asserts `MAX(position) = K` over `tx_meta FOR ALL VALID_TIME` (`packages/xtdb/src/XtdbStorageAdapter.ts:189-196`), so all
    13	commits in a scope serialize on one counter, and that assertion may scan more rows as history grows. When this lands, XTDB has a
    14	measured commit path whose CAS is per subject, whose order and gap-freedom come from XTDB's own log, and whose feed carries
    15	(tx, entity, attribute, valid-time range) to a durable consumer checkpoint, with numbers for both against the A0 control.
    16	
    17	## Point of view (the technique to pursue)
    18	1. **Per-subject CAS.** The command asserts the subject's last position (`ASSERT EXISTS (... _id = subject AND pos = n)` or NOT EXISTS
    19	   for a new subject; R16 measured about +5 ms), never a scope-wide maximum. The idempotency claim stays an `_id` row keyed by commandId
    20	   (R16: duplicates rejected 100/100 with P0004, receipt readable by `_id` in 11 ms); read the claim before writing, so a duplicate costs
    21	   a read, not a rejected transaction.
    22	2. **Order and gap-freedom from the log.** XTDB applies transactions in log order and tx ids are strictly increasing log offsets (R16 3.4).
    23	   Use the tx id (or the tx's system time, if you show it is strictly increasing per node on 2.2.0) as the feed position, with a
    24	   high-water mark ("every tx at or below H is committed or aborted") replacing the dense counter. Write a short design note on what
    25	   C1's basis rules need from a non-dense position and keep `recordedPosition` meaning "this commit's place in the total order".
    26	3. **Feed carrier in the same transaction.** `METADATA = {cmd, subj, pos, attrs, vf, vt}` plus a feed row inserted in the same
    27	   transaction (R16 3.4). One tailer on `xt.source_log_msgs` (O(window), 2 ms empty poll), commit status from `xt.txs` or
    28	   `xt.replica_log_msgs`, fan-out in Triplex. Never many pollers on `xt.txs` (R16: 32 pollers stalled commits for seconds).
    29	4. **Durable consumption.** The consumer writes its affected-subject set and its checkpoint (`msg_id`) in one durable write; effects are
    30	   keyed (consumer, position, subject). Inject crashes before the effect, after the effect before the checkpoint, and after the checkpoint;
    31	   count replay attempts and visible duplicate effects separately (the control arm without keyed effects should show at least one
    32	   duplicate, which proves the test can fail).
    33	5. **Two arms, same trace.** A0: today's adapter (scope-wide counter). Candidate: points 1 to 4 behind an adapter option, default off.
    34	   Same seed, same open-loop offer schedule (TARGETS 2.1; use EXP-N01's generator from `exp/N01-northstar-bench` if it is ready, else
    35	   a minimal Poisson generator with the 2.1 parameters and bind its hash in the receipt).
    36	
