    92	### 1.3 Implication for P1
    93	
    94	- Per-node read capacity is bounded by the CPUs given to the JVM. A 256-reader goal needs followers (Kafka and an object store) or many more CPUs, plus `numThreads` raised.
    95	- `readOnlyPort` gives a second pool, so reader connection storms cannot starve writer connections of pool slots. It does not separate CPU.
    96	- Keep reads per write low: batch with `_id IN (...)` (50 ids per query gave 1,321 subjects/s on one connection, section 5).
    97	
    98	**Open:** 256 and 1,024 readers; follower node; `numThreads` raised; a dedicated machine.
    99	
   100	## 2. Q2 (P2): small per-subject commits
   101	
   102	### 2.1 Measured (`p2.out`, command shape in section 0.3, one connection unless noted)
   103	
   104	| Case | n | p50 | p90 | p99 | Notes |
   105	| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
   106	| 10 facts, 1 connection, closed loop | 550 | 23.9 ms | 56.6 | 201.5 | 29 commits/s |
   107	| 5 facts | 270 | 52.9 | 84.9 | 158.9 | run right after the first; noisy |
   108	| 50 facts | 270 | 31.1 | 60.6 | 84.2 | |
   109	| 10 facts, 4 connections, closed loop | 1,000 | 44.0 | 66.4 | 88.9 | 88 commits/s, 881 facts/s |
   110	| 10 facts, 16 connections, closed loop | 4,000 | 89.5 | 129.3 | 176.2 | 172 commits/s, 1,722 facts/s |
   111	| 10 facts, open loop 40/s, no readers (`p1b.out`) | 602 | 19.6 | 61.7 | 105.7 | service time; 0 errors |
   112	| 10 facts, open loop 40/s, 1 reader (`p1.out`) | 801 | 17.2 | n/a | 41.7 | service time |
   113	
   114	Per-statement cost on a quiet moment (`misc-raw.txt`, 1 connection, p50): single-row insert transaction 14.7 ms, plus `ASSERT NOT EXISTS` by `_id` 15.3 ms, plus CAS assert 20.7 ms (`ASSERT EXISTS (... _id AND pos = n)` is the same, 20.4 ms), 10-column `UPDATE` alone 20.6 ms, 10-row multi-row insert alone 6.8 ms. The floor is a commit round trip; the CAS assert adds about 5 ms.
   115	
   116	### 2.2 Correctness (all 100%)
   117	
   118	| Check | Result |
   119	| --- | --- |
   120	| Read-your-own-write, same connection | 200/200 |
   121	| Read on a different connection with no token | 200/200 (the docs do not guarantee this, `txs-in-xtdb.md:40-66`) |
   122	| Different connection after `SHOW AWAIT_TOKEN` and `SET AWAIT_TOKEN` | 200/200 |
   123	| Duplicate `commandId` (lost-ACK retry) | 100/100 rejected with SQLSTATE `P0004` and message `dup`; receipt readable by `_id` in 11 ms; exactly 10 ledger rows for the command |
   124	| CAS race (two commands, same expected `pos`) | one commits, the loser gets `P0004` `cas`, no partial rows |
   125	
   126	### 2.3 `COMMIT SYNC` versus await tokens
   127	
   128	`COMMIT SYNC` returns after the transaction is indexed on the connected node (R1 Q5/Q6). `ASYNC = true` returns earlier and a later read on the same connection waits (R7 Q4). Await tokens only matter for another node or process. Per H19 in R14, the token advances on writes only, so pass an explicit snapshot token across processes.
   129	
   130	### 2.4 Implication for P2
   131	
   132	- Correct today: atomicity, CAS, idempotency, receipts.
   133	- The bar (p50 10 ms, p99 50 ms) is not met here: p50 16 to 24 ms, p99 33 to 200 ms, on a loaded host. The single-row floor of 15 ms in the quiet breakdown suggests the host, not the statement count, sets p50. A dedicated run could move this by a large factor; I cannot say how far.
   134	- Throughput is not the constraint: 16 writers sustained 172 commits/s (1,722 facts/s), 2.3x the burst rate of 750 facts/s.
   135	- Idempotency by `_id` of a claims table is a cheap `_id` lookup (about 11 ms read, about 12 ms rejected duplicate). A duplicate costs a full rejected transaction; a read of the claim row first would avoid the write path.
   136	
   137	## 3. Q3 (P3): change feed
   138	
   139	### 3.1 What XTDB 2.2.0 exposes
   140	
   141	| Mechanism | What it gives | What it lacks | Evidence |
   142	| --- | --- | --- | --- |
   143	| `xt.txs` | One row per transaction: `_id` (equal to the source-log message id), `committed`, `error`, `system_time`, `user_metadata` (the `METADATA` map). | Entity, attribute, valid-time range. Range queries (`_id > x`) scan the table. | probe, R1 Q6 |
   144	| `xt.source_log_msgs` | Ordered raw source-log messages by `msg_id` (log offset). Requires `msg_id` bounds. Includes `tx_ops` decoded to EDN. | Parameterised statements show the SQL text with Arrow argument bytes (`:args #bytes "ffff..."`), not values. Aborted transactions appear (commit status is not in the source log). Offsets must be exact record starts or the read fails with "log file corrupted ... expected record separator". | `core/src/main/clojure/xtdb/log_tables.clj:32-37,217-234` |
   145	| `xt.replica_log_msgs` | Resolved messages: `resolved-tx` with `tx-id`, `committed`, `error`, `system-time`, `table-names`. | No row data, no metadata. Its offsets differ from tx ids. | same file, probe |
   146	| Kafka replica topic (`${topic}-replica`) | Leader's resolved output, tailed by followers. | An internal format, not a documented consumer API. Not tested (no Kafka). | `docs/.../ops/config/log/kafka.md` |
   147	| External sources (Postgres CDC, Kafka Connect) | Inbound only: they feed an XTDB secondary database. | No outbound CDC from XTDB. | `docs/.../ops/external-sources/overview.md` |
   148	
   149	Verdict: no supported change stream. A feed with (tx, entity, attribute, valid-time range) must be written by Triplex.
   150	
   151	### 3.2 Measured tail cost (`p3.out`, 18,514 transactions in `xt.txs`, 1 client)
   152	
   153	| Query | Rows | p50 |
   154	| --- | --- | --- |
   155	| `xt.txs WHERE _id > tip-5000 ... LIMIT 100` | 1 | 13.9 ms |
   156	| `xt.txs WHERE _id > 0 ... LIMIT 100` (far back) | 100 | 23.1 ms |
   157	| `xt.txs WHERE _id > tip` (empty poll) | 0 | 11.9 ms |
   158	| `xt.source_log_msgs` window of 2,001 messages | 2,001 | 46.0 ms |
   159	| `xt.source_log_msgs` 101 messages with `tx_ops` | 101 | 38.6 ms |
   160	| `xt.source_log_msgs` empty poll at the tip | 1 | 2.1 ms |
   161	| `xt.replica_log_msgs` first 200 KB | 1,513 | 12.7 ms |
   162	
   163	`xt.txs` polls cost a table scan (12 ms at 18.5k rows, growing with the table). The source-log tail is O(window) from an offset: an empty poll is 2 ms.
